{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791567913,"nonce":"KLfzMa96opOvfG+M/9deNpkoU9k=","message":"Correction and the core widgets. The changes made so far are in blinc_layout, the core crate, and benefit core widgets and cn alike. cn was only the first set of closures read.\n\nCorrection to the inventory above: blinc_layout's 63 .on_state( sites are mostly the API's own documentation and tests (stateful.rs 28 doc comments and 12 tests, div.rs 5 doc comments, no real code in either). The real core-widget sites are about eleven: toggle, checkbox, radio, input_otp, text_area, code, rich_text_editor, motion and three in widgets/media. The 31 in blinc_cn are real code.\n\nCore widgets fall into three kinds:\n1. Style chosen by state: toggle, checkbox, radio (and button). Each closure reads hover or pressed state and the on/checked signal, then recomputes colours by hand, and re-reads the stylesheet itself through apply_css_overrides. That function exists in six core files (button, checkbox, radio, toggle, text_input, text_area): a parallel copy of the CSS cascade inside each widget. This is the pattern the CSS-first direction removes, and it ties to 7cabafe (component defaults as the lowest tier).\n2. Editing widgets driven by a state machine: text_input, text_area, code, rich_text_editor, input_otp. Legitimate logic, with visuals mixed in; case by case, last.\n3. Structural: the three media closures (251, 67 and 15 lines).\n\nWhat that means for the order:\n- The signal-to-content primitives built so far (bound text, bound font properties, show/hide) cover the display-only closures, mostly in cn.\n- Core toggle, checkbox and radio restyle on a signal plus hover and pressed. The signal part maps onto bound background, border colour and opacity, which have writers. Hover and pressed need the per-node interaction signals (step 5) or CSS state selectors, so they come after that.","files":null}]}